    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Judaism is the religion of the people of Israel, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> tells of one God who made a covenant with them and gave them the Torah, the law and teaching that guides Jewish life.</li><li>After the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 70 CE, the rabbis rebuilt Judaism around the synagogue, prayer and the study of the Torah and the Talmud, allowing it to survive in dispersion across the world.</li><li>Through the centuries of exile, persecution and the Holocaust, and with the founding of the State of Israel in 1948, it has remained the faith of about 15 million people and the root from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> grew.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Dead Sea Scrolls, found in caves near Qumran from 1947, include copies of books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> about a thousand years older than the oldest complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> known before them.</p></aside>
    </section>

