    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moses"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moses.jpg" alt="Moses" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_079.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rembrandt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Judaism is the religion of the Jewish people, and its story begins with the story the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> tells of them. In the biblical narrative, God made a covenant with Abraham, promising him descendants and a land; his grandson Jacob, renamed Israel, was the father of twelve tribes; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, enslaved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, were freed under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, received the Torah from God at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> and entered the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. Historians debate how much of this account reflects events, and there is little evidence outside the Bible for the period before the monarchy.</p><p>The people of Israel appear in history in the late second millennium BCE: an Egyptian inscription of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>, around 1208 BCE, names “Israel” as a people in Canaan. According to the Bible, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> the capital of a united kingdom in the 10th century BCE and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> built the First Temple there. After Solomon the kingdom divided into Israel in the north, conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> in 722 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the south, centred on Jerusalem.</p><p>Israelite religion was at first one of several cults in the land, and many scholars think the worship of one God alone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>, took shape gradually, through the prophets who denounced the worship of other gods and through the reforms of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> of Judah in the late 7th century BCE. The decisive crisis came in 586 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> destroyed Jerusalem and the Temple and deported many Judeans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. In exile, without Temple or king, they preserved their identity through their scriptures, the Sabbath and the observance of the law.</p><p>When the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Babylon in 539 BCE, he allowed the exiles to return, and the Temple was rebuilt, completed by tradition in 516 BCE. In the following centuries, under Persian, Greek and then Roman rule, the scriptures were gathered and edited and the religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period developed, with its priests, its scribes and its rival schools: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a> and others.</p><p>From this time the people of Judah, the Judeans or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, also lived in large communities outside the land, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, Egypt and across the Greek and Roman world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maimonides"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maimonides.jpg" alt="Maimonides" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Moses_Maimonides_in_Thesaurus_antiquitatum_sacrarum.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blaisio Ugolino, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the centre of Judaism is belief in one God, creator of the world, who is just and merciful, who revealed himself to Israel and who acts in history. The Shema, recited morning and evening, declares: “Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is one.” God is not represented by any image.</p><p>Judaism understands the relationship between God and the Jewish people as a covenant, a binding agreement: God chose Israel and gave them the Torah, and Israel is to live by its commandments, the mitzvot. Rabbinic tradition counts 613 commandments in the Torah, covering worship, ethics, family life, food, the calendar and justice. Among them are the observance of the Sabbath, the day of rest from Friday evening to Saturday evening; the dietary laws of kashrut; and the duties of charity, justice and love of one’s neighbour.</p><p>Jewish tradition places practice at the centre of religious life more than formal statements of belief, though thinkers have defined principles of faith, most famously the thirteen principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> in the 12th century, among them the existence and unity of God, the prophecy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, the coming of the Messiah and the resurrection of the dead. The Messiah, in Jewish expectation, is a future king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>’s line who will restore Israel and bring peace to the world.</p><p>The Jewish year is marked by festivals. Rosh Hashanah, the new year, and Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, a day of fasting and repentance, fall in the autumn; the pilgrimage festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, which recalls the exodus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Shavuot, which celebrates the giving of the Torah, and Sukkot, the festival of booths, recall the journey of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a> and Purim remember later deliverances. Rites of passage include circumcision of boys on the eighth day after birth, the bar and bat mitzvah at which young people take on the commandments, marriage under a canopy and mourning rites for the dead.</p><p>Modern Judaism includes several movements: Orthodox Judaism, which holds that the Torah and its rabbinic interpretation are divinely given and binding; Reform or Liberal Judaism, which emphasises ethics and adapts practice to modern life; and Conservative or Masorti Judaism between them, as well as many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who identify with the people and its culture rather than its observance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible.jpg" alt="Hebrew Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The scripture of Judaism is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, called the Tanakh from the initials of its three parts: the Torah, the five books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>; the Nevi’im, the Prophets, which include the histories of Israel from Joshua to the kings and the books of prophets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> and Ezekiel; and the Ketuvim, the Writings, among them the Psalms, Proverbs, Job and Daniel. Written mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, with parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, it was composed and edited over many centuries and its contents were settled by the early centuries CE.</p><p>The Torah, in the narrow sense of the five books, is read in the synagogue from a handwritten scroll in a cycle that runs through the year. In the wider sense, Torah means all of God’s teaching, including the Oral Torah, the traditions of interpretation that rabbinic Judaism holds were given to Moses together with the written text.</p><p>The Oral Torah was set down in writing after the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>. Around 200 CE Rabbi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> ha-Nasi compiled the Mishnah, a code of Jewish law arranged in six orders. Generations of rabbis in the academies of the land of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> discussed and expanded it in the Gemara; together the Mishnah and Gemara form the Talmud, of which the Babylonian Talmud, completed around 600, became the authoritative work of Jewish law and learning. Collections of interpretation and story, the Midrash, explored the meaning of the biblical text.</p><p>Medieval scholars wrote commentaries and codes that are still studied. Rashi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, in the 11th century, wrote the most widely used commentary on the Bible and Talmud; Moses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>, in 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, wrote the Mishneh Torah, a code of all Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, a work of philosophy; Joseph Karo’s Shulchan Aruch of 1565 became the standard code of practice. The Zohar, written in 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, became the central text of the mystical tradition of Kabbalah.</p><p>The prayer book, the siddur, gathers the liturgy of daily, Sabbath and festival prayer, much of it from the early rabbinic period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba.jpg" alt="Simon bar Kokhba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kokhba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Szyk_(1894-1951)._Bar_Kochba_(1927),_Paris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arthur Szyk, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived outside the land of Israel from the time of the Babylonian exile, and by the Roman period there were large communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In Alexandria the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> scriptures were translated into Greek, the version known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>.</p><p>Roman rule in Judea ended in disaster. The Jewish revolt that broke out in 66 CE was crushed in 70, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>; a second revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kokhba</a> in 132–135 was crushed in turn, and Jews were barred from Jerusalem. The centre of Jewish life moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and to Babylonia, whose academies led the Jewish world for centuries.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Jewish communities spread across the Islamic world and Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Jewish culture flourished in poetry, philosophy and science, before persecution under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in the 12th century and the Christian reconquest. In Christian Europe Jews faced periods of tolerance and of savage persecution: massacres during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, accusations of ritual murder, confinement to ghettos and expulsions, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1290, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 14th century and Spain in 1492. The Sephardi Jews expelled from Spain settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the Netherlands; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a> of northern Europe moved east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, which became the largest centre of Jewish life by the 17th century.</p><p>From the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and the emancipation of Jews in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> brought civic rights and new movements of reform. In the 19th and early 20th centuries millions of Jews emigrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> called for a Jewish national home in the land of Israel.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, between 1941 and 1945, Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and its collaborators murdered about six million Jews, two-thirds of the Jews of Europe. In 1948 the State of Israel was founded; Jews from Arab countries, from Europe and later from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> settled there. Today about 15 million Jews live in the world, most of them in Israel and the United States.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/safed"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safed.jpg" alt="Safed" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Safed1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Beny Shlevich, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the destruction of the Temple, Jewish religious life came to centre on the synagogue, the home and the study of the Torah. The synagogue, a house of prayer, reading and study, needs no priest: any community with ten adult men, in Orthodox practice, or ten adults, in most liberal congregations, may hold full public prayer. The rabbi, a teacher of the law rather than a priest, interprets the tradition and guides the community.</p><p>The home is equally important. The Sabbath begins on Friday evening with candles, blessings over wine and bread and a family meal; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> Seder, a ritual meal at which the story of the exodus is retold, is celebrated at home. Many households keep kosher kitchens.</p><p>Through most of its history Jewish life was organised in self-governing communities that ran their own courts, schools, charities and burial societies, collected taxes for the rulers under whom they lived and negotiated their rights and protection. Learning was a religious duty, and the communities maintained schools and academies, the yeshivot, where men studied Talmud.</p><p>Religious movements grew within these communities. Kabbalah, the mystical tradition, developed in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Provence and was renewed in 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a> by Isaac Luria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasidism">Hasidism</a>, founded in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by the Baal Shem Tov, taught joyful devotion and gathered followers around charismatic leaders; its opponents defended the primacy of Talmudic study. The Haskalah, the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, encouraged secular learning.</p><p>Jewish communities have spoken many languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, Arabic, Judaeo-Spanish (Ladino) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, the language of the Ashkenazim, while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as the language of prayer and study; modern Hebrew was revived as a spoken language in the late 19th and early 20th centuries and is the language of Israel.</p><p>The roles of women in religious life have been a subject of change: liberal movements ordain women as rabbis, while Orthodox communities maintain traditional distinctions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Temple in Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Modell_BW_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berthold Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Judaism is one of the oldest continuous religious traditions in the world and the parent of two others: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, which began as a movement within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> Judaism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which honours Abraham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and the prophets of Israel. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, as the Christian Old Testament, became the scripture of half the world, and its ideas of one God, of a moral law binding on all, of the dignity of every human being made in God’s image and of history moving toward redemption shaped the civilisations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><p>The weekly day of rest, the Ten Commandments, the prophetic call for justice for the poor and the stranger and the vision of a world at peace have entered the moral language of much of humanity. Jewish thinkers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-alexandria">Philo of Alexandria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> to Spinoza, Moses Mendelssohn and Martin Buber, contributed to philosophy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> have been prominent far beyond their numbers in modern science, medicine, literature, music and scholarship.</p><p>The survival of the Jewish people through two thousand years of dispersion, without a state for most of that time, is one of the remarkable facts of history. It was sustained by the Torah and its study, by communal institutions and by the hope of return expressed in the prayer “next year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>”.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">The Holocaust</a> stands as the central catastrophe of modern Jewish history and a defining moral lesson of the 20th century; its memory shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and human rights. The founding of the State of Israel in 1948 created a Jewish state for the first time in nearly two thousand years, and the conflict over the land with the Palestinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> remains unresolved.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Judaism, the religion of the Jewish people, is founded on the covenant between the one God and Israel and on the Torah, the written and oral teaching that guides Jewish life. Rebuilt by the rabbis after the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> in 70 CE around the synagogue and the Talmud, it survived exile and persecution across the world and is the root from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> grew.</em></p>

